     ffdd, ssttddiinn, ssttddoouutt, ssttddeerrrr — file descriptor files

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The files _/_d_e_v_/_f_d_/_0 through _/_d_e_v_/_f_d_/_# refer to file descriptors which can
     be accessed through the file system.  If the file descriptor is open and
     the mode the file is being opened with is a subset of the mode of the ex‐
     isting descriptor, the call:

           fd = open("/dev/fd/0", mode);

     and the call:

           fd = fcntl(0, F_DUPFD, 0);

     are equivalent.

     Opening the files _/_d_e_v_/_s_t_d_i_n, _/_d_e_v_/_s_t_d_o_u_t and _/_d_e_v_/_s_t_d_e_r_r is equivalent
     to the following calls:

           fd = fcntl(STDIN_FILENO,  F_DUPFD, 0);
           fd = fcntl(STDOUT_FILENO, F_DUPFD, 0);
           fd = fcntl(STDERR_FILENO, F_DUPFD, 0);

     Flags to the open(2) call other than O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY and O_RDWR are
     ignored.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     By default, _/_d_e_v_/_f_d is provided by devfs(5), which provides nodes for the
     first three file descriptors.  Some sites may require nodes for addi‐
     tional file descriptors; these can be made available by mounting
     fdescfs(5) on _/_d_e_v_/_f_d.

